- `#address f` — prints a friendly "address card" for any declaration (works on your code and on Mathlib, e.g. `#address Nat.add`).
- `#compareaddress f g` — puts two declarations side by side and reports which coordinates they share (e.g. your code vs a Mathlib lemma).
- `#ward f` — scans the loaded environment (Mathlib included) and lists declarations in the same mod-47 ward — your nearest Mathlib neighbours (e.g. it surfaced `Matrix.smul_hadamard`, `Finset.subsetSum_mono`).

Proved theorems (no `sorry`, only the standard axioms `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`):
- `c71_lt`, `c59_lt`, `c47_lt`, `slot_lt` — every coordinate is a valid grid cell;
- `addressOf_addr` — the address is computed exactly from the fingerprint;
- `sameAddress_equivalence` — "shares an address" is a genuine equivalence relation, so addresses partition all code (yours + Mathlib) into classes;
- `sameAddress_unique` — within one 196883-cell page the address is a perfect identifier (same address ⇒ same declaration).

## New n00b documentation — `USER_ADDRESS_GUIDE.md`
A beginner guide requiring no prior knowledge of the project: the TL;DR answer to your question, install/import instructions, copy-paste examples for all three commands (with real verified output), a use-cases/"skills" table (stable IDs, structural-duplicate detection, finding Mathlib neighbours, addressing a domain-specific language), a worked DSL mini-example, a list of what is formally guaranteed, and an honest scope note.

