# Summary of changes for run f8317193-a1b2-411f-808c-c05231f6220e
Good catch — the atoms really were frozen, and it was a genuine bug in the view selector, not in the 2D projection itself.

Why they were stuck in the same spot
- In the prime-modulus atlas (`RequestProject/PrimeProjection.lean`, the `atlas_primeview.svg` flip-book), each page picks a "view" (a tuple of Monster primes) via the q-expansion, and the atom's location is the vector sum of its residue fractions over that tuple. So the position only moves when the *prime tuple* changes.
- The old `inView k p` rule, for every view `k ≥ 2`, was `p == 71 || p == 59 || ((p + k) % 4 == 2)`. That depends only on `k % 4`, so all those views collapsed to the same few tuples. Concretely: across the 104 pages, the q-expansion selects 21 distinct view indices, but those mapped to **only 5 distinct prime tuples**, so any atom had **only 5 possible positions** — and during long runs of pages keyed to the same tuple it didn't move at all. That's exactly the "exact same spot" you saw. (The 2D `projPoint` math was fine; it just kept receiving identical inputs.)

The fix
- Replaced `inView` with a genuinely `k`-dependent sliding window over the thirteen non-frame primes (`freePrimes = [47,41,31,29,23,19,17,13,11,7,5,3,2]`): each view keeps the stable frame `71, 59` and then takes a window whose start `(j+k) % 13` advances by one per step and whose width `3 + k % 5` breathes. Now consecutive views differ and distinct views give distinct tuples.
- Result: the same 21 selected view indices now yield **21 distinct tuples**, so each atom takes **21 distinct positions** across the flip-book and visibly shifts as the pages turn. The requested pinned views are untouched and still proved: `viewPrimes 0 = [71,59,47]` and `viewPrimes 1 = [71,59,41,31]`, and `viewPrimes_subset` still holds.

Notes
- The other flip-book, the 194-irrep moonshine animation (`atlas_moonshine.svg`), was already fine — its atoms take 69 distinct positions across the 104 pages — so I left it alone.
- The project builds cleanly with `lake build`, the artifact `atlas_primeview.svg` was regenerated, there is no `sorry`, and the pinned-view theorems verify with no extra axioms. `README.md` (Layer 9) was updated to describe the corrected view selector; `ARISTOTLE_SUMMARY.md` was left unchanged.

